1
00:00:00.287 --> 00:00:09.399
As you just saw, the core idea of RAG is to pair an off-the-shelf LLM with a knowledge base of information it might not have had access to when it was trained.

2
00:00:09.399 --> 00:00:14.699
Many LLM-powered systems implement this model, so let's explore a few of them now.

3
00:00:14.699 --> 00:00:18.799
One important application of RAG is for code generation.

4
00:00:18.799 --> 00:00:33.200
This is significant, because while language models have been trained on lots of code, conceivably every public Git repository, generating correct code for a specific project requires specialized information.

5
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:42.099
The LLM needs to know the classes, functions, and definitions in the project, as well as the overall coding style being used.

6
00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:50.400
Building a RAG system that uses your own code base as the knowledge base makes this information available to an LLM.

7
00:00:50.400 --> 00:01:01.599
By retrieving relevant classes, definitions, and files from your repository, an LLM is much better able to generate code or answer questions relevant to your project.

8
00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:07.199
Another major application is customizing chatbots to an individual company.

9
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.400
Every company has its own products, policies, and communication guidelines.

10
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:20.500
Treating these enterprise documents as a knowledge base allows you to deploy LLMs in a variety of useful ways.

11
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:29.199
You could build a customer service chatbot that knows information about your company's products, current inventory, or common troubleshooting steps.

12
00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:37.699
You could deploy an internal chatbot that accurately answers questions about company policies or direct you towards useful documentation.

13
00:01:37.699 --> 00:01:51.599
In either case, the knowledge base helps ground the LLM's responses in your company's specific products or policies, and helps minimize instances where the LLM generates generic or misleading responses.

14
00:01:51.599 --> 00:01:55.800
RAG has significant applications in healthcare and legal domains.

15
00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:04.400
In these instances, the knowledge base might contain legal documents from a particular case, text from recently published medical journals, and so forth.

16
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.699
In fields like these, in which precision is imperative and the amount of niche and potentially private information is vast,

17
00:02:11.699 --> 00:02:20.300
a RAG-based approach might be the only way to deploy an LLM-based product that is sufficiently accurate and uses private information.

18
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:25.500
Another major application is an AI-assisted web search.

19
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:32.500
Historically, search engines have worked like a retriever. Given a search query, they return relevant websites.

20
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:42.000
Today, search engines will provide AI summaries of these search results to quickly present the most useful information in a skimmable way.

21
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.199
These AI web summaries are basically a RAG system whose knowledge base is the entire Internet.

22
00:02:49.199 --> 00:02:55.000
While large-scale RAG systems can be powerful, the same is true for highly personalized ones.

23
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:04.300
Personalized assistants in your text messages, email client, word processor, calendar, and so forth are now able to help you send messages,

24
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:10.000
organize your schedule, draft documents, and complete projects big and small.

25
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:17.800
In each case, the more context the LLM has about the project you're working on, the better they're able to support that work.

26
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:27.000
In these cases, the knowledge base might be relatively small. For example, your text messages, contact lists, emails, or a folder of documents.

27
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:34.500
These kinds of documents are dense with important context, so a RAG system with access to this small-scale personal information

28
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.300
can complete tasks in a way that is significantly more relevant to what you're doing.

29
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.399
As you can see, the RAG model has applicability in a wide variety of contexts.

30
00:03:44.399 --> 00:03:54.500
Many companies, organizations, and even you have collections of information that can be used to enhance the quality of text generated by an LLM.

31
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:59.800
Whenever you have access to information that likely wasn't used to train a large-language model,

32
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.800
there is a potential for building a useful RAG application.

33
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:09.500
They might even allow LLMs to be used in contexts that were otherwise impossible.

34
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:17.000
As you continue through this course, hopefully you'll start seeing opportunities in your work and life where a RAG approach would be useful.

35
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.500
For now though, join me in the next video and let's dive deeper into the architecture of a RAG system.

